WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.634
This is your first print: a cube exactly 2.000 in on every side. In Onshape, click Create → Document.

00:00:09.184 --> 00:00:14.060
Name it “Calibration Cube – Your Name.” Then click Create.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:20.566
You land in a blank Part Studio with three planes: Top, Front, and Right.

00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:28.477
Check the units: document menu → Workspace units. Length should say Inch, with 3 decimals.

00:00:29.027 --> 00:00:35.192
Start a sketch on the Top plane and press N. Press R and draw a center-point rectangle on the origin.

00:00:35.742 --> 00:00:40.676
Press D and pick the right edge. The box shows the size you happened to draw.

00:00:41.226 --> 00:00:44.825
Type 2.000 and press Enter.

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:52.573
Do the same for the top edge. Now it's a 2 × 2 square, and the lines are black: fully defined.

00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:57.558
Finish the sketch. Press Shift+E to extrude, and click the square.

00:00:58.108 --> 00:01:02.950
Set the depth to 2.000 in, and click the green check.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.579
There's your cube.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:14.162
Before you submit, verify. Click one edge. Bottom right of the screen: Length 2.000 in.

00:01:14.712 --> 00:01:16.140
Check the second direction.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:22.948
And the height. All three read 2.000 in. That's your designed number for the accuracy log.

00:01:23.498 --> 00:01:28.281
To submit, right-click the part → Export. Put your name in the file name.

00:01:28.831 --> 00:01:44.632
One trap: for STL, change Units to Millimeter. Slicers read STL numbers as mm, so an inch file prints a tiny 2 mm cube. Or pick 3MF, which keeps the units.
